<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Briviesca</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Virovesca</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Briviesca is a town in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a>, in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, on the road from Burgos to Miranda de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a>. It descends from Virovesca, a town of the Autrigones named by Pliny and Ptolemy that became a Roman stopping place on the road through the upper Ebro region. In 1387 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cortes-of-castile">Cortes of Castile</a> met there under John I.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/briviesca.jpg" alt="Briviesca" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: José Luís Sagredo · CC BY-SA 3.0 es · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ex-Colegiata_de_Santa_Mar%C3%ADa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Briviesca is the capital of the comarca of La Bureba, in the north-east of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and León. It lies halfway between Burgos and Miranda de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a>, in the valley of the river Oca between the mountains of San José and of Los Pinos, at 718 metres above sea level. The valley opens onto the extensive plain of La Bureba, which is often called the granary of Castile because of its cereal production. The origin of the name is not known exactly. It seems to be of proto-Indo-European origin and to mean the head or regional capital, from the root uiro- and the suffix -uesca.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Prehistoric finds in the zone show human presence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paleolithic">Paleolithic</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> remains turn up at Briviesca and nearby places. From the 3rd century BCE the Autrigones occupied a large area of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> valley, and the first settlements at Briviesca date from the 1st century BCE, when they established their capital there. The first document to refer to it is the Natural History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a>, who cites Virovesca as the capital of the Autrigones. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> it was an important population centre at the crossroads of two Roman roads.</p><p>This location, and the medieval boom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camino-de-santiago">Camino de Santiago</a>, made the town grow gradually until its maximum splendour in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. A Jewish community was documented from 1240 and lasted until the expulsion of 1492. The town was not always where it is now: it stood in the nearby mountains, where defence mattered most, until the fourteenth century, when Blanca of Portugal, granddaughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-x-of-castile">Alfonso the Wise</a>, bought it and moved it to its present site because the new location gave better access and trade.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Briviesca is a municipality of five settlements: Briviesca itself, Cameno, Quintanillabón, Revillagodos and Valdazo. It had 6,948 inhabitants in 2016 and is the head of the judicial district of Briviesca. Its monumental complex includes the Collegiate Church of Santa María la Mayor, the Monastery of Santa Clara with its Gothic cloister, the cloister of the Hospitalillo, the typically Castilian Plaza Mayor and the Church of San Martín. The sanctuary of Santa Casilda lies close by. Its arms show two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a> and a Christian, which are explained as a sign of coexistence between cultures.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Briviesca” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They lived in hillforts and lowland settlements, farming and herding, with towns such as <b>Virovesca</b> (Briviesca) and Tritium Autrigonum named by Pliny and Ptolemy.</blockquote><a href="/state/autrigones">Autrigones</a></li></ul></section>
